INCOMPLETE AS PORTED — cobble validated this against graph building only; it never lowers to IR.

red-dragon's condition_lowering.py has two silent fallbacks for an unrecognised ExprNode:
  logger.warning('Unknown expression node type: %s', ...); return ctx.const_to_reg(0)
and in _expr_node_to_arg_dict:
  return {'kind': 'ref', 'name': getattr(node, 'name', '')}

So adding FigurativeNode/LengthOfNode/DfhRespNode to make the ASG parse, without teaching the lowering what they mean, trades a loud ValueError for silently wrong IR: a figurative constant lowers to 0 (accidentally correct for ZEROS, wrong for SPACES, HIGH-VALUES, LOW-VALUES and ALL literals), and LENGTH OF becomes a reference to a field named ''.

Definition of done therefore includes the lowering, not just the parse:
- figurative constants lower to the right fill value per receiving item (SPACES, HIGH-VALUES, LOW-VALUES, ZEROS, ALL <lit>), not to 0;
- LENGTH OF lowers to the compile-time byte count;
- DFHRESP resolves through the existing dfhresp prepass rather than becoming a field ref;
- an ExprNode the lowering does not handle should fail loudly rather than returning const 0 — the current fallback is what makes this class of bug silent.

Tests must cover a lowered/executed path, not only expr_from_dict round-tripping.
